2026-06-30 15:13:33,989 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-e3e38306-af48-4bad-b6a9-955a65536936', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\n161206_HsP_90_1.raw\n161206_HsP_90_2.raw\n161206_HsP_90_ADP_1.raw\n161206_HsP_90_ADP_2.raw\n161206_HsP_90_ATPgS_1.raw\n161206_HsP_90_ATPgS_2.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nCharacterizing protein conformers by cross-linking mass spectrometry and pattern recognition\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nMotivation: Chemical cross-linking coupled to mass spectrometry (XLMS) emerged as a powerful technique for studying protein structures and large-scale protein-protein interactions. Nonetheless, XLMS lacks software tailored toward dealing with multiple conformers; this scenario can lead to high-quality identifications that are mutually exclusive. This limitation hampers the applicability of XLMS in structural experiments of dynamic protein systems, where less abundant conformers of the target protein are expected in sample. Results: We present QUIN-XL, a software that uses unsupervised clustering to group cross-link identifications by their quantitative profile across multiple samples. QUIN-XL highlights regions of the protein or system presenting changes in its conformation when comparing different biological conditions. We demonstrate the usefulness of our software by revisiting the HSP90 protein, comparing three of its different conformers. QUIN-XL’s clusters correlate directly to known protein 3D structures of the conformers and therefore validates our software.\n\n[METHODS]\nThe HSP90 protein used for the experiments was the human one, analyzed in a purified solution with the nucleotides being added separately afterwards for the formation of complexes. This way, each of the samples should be enriched for a different conformer. The nucleotides were ADP and ATP-γ-S (referenced in the text as ATP), which is a more stable analog of ATP and does not hydrolyze immediately. The cross-linker used was DSS dissolved in DMF (final concentration of 27mmol/L) in a molar excess of 100 times relative to the protein concentration. The cross-link reactions occurred for 2 hours at 27°C. Reducing of the disulfide bonds was done by adding 20μL of a 10mmol/L solution of DTT (prepared in a 100mmol/L buffer solution of ammonium bicarbonate) and incubating it for 30 minutes at 70°C. Alkylation of thiol groups was done by adding 20μL of a 50mmol/L solution of iodoacetamide (prepared in a 100mmol/L ammonium bicarbonate buffer solution) and incubating it for 30 minutes in the dark at room temperature. The cross-linked proteins were digested using trypsin in a proportion of 1:50 (w/w) enzyme:substrate for 18 hours at 37°C. Mass spectrometry data for HSP90 was generated in the following conditions: a) ATP-bound, b) ADP-bound, and c) nucleotide-free (Apo). Digested samples sample were analyzed in two technical replicates using an Orbitrap Fusion Lumos Tribrid (ThermoFisher Scientific) mass spectrometer coupled with an Easy nLC 1200 chromatography system. Each sample was analyzed in 40 minutes chromatography, using linear gradients set as follows: 5 to 30% B (v/v)  (with solvent B: acetonitrile + 0.1% formic acid (v/v)) from 0 to 20 minutes, 30 to 90%  B from 20 to 35 minutes, then 90% B from 35 to 40 minutes. Instrument method was set up in DDA mode, HCD fragmentation with stepped collision energy from 30 to 34. Survey scans were acquired at resolution 60,000. Precursor selection was performed by cycle time with maximum injection time of 250 ms, and 8 seconds dynamic exclusion window. Precursor ions with charge 3+ to 8+ were selected for fragmentation. MS2 spectra were acquired with resolution 30,000.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to cluster_0 unless the publication clearly states they are part of the biological experiment.\n\n7. If all biological files appear to come from one condition, create only cluster_1 plus cluster_0 if there are unassigned/QC files.\n\n8. Confidence guidelines:\n   - "high": filenames and publication/metadata agree clearly\n   - "medium": assignment is likely but partially inferred from filenames or incomplete metadata\n   - "low": weak evidence, ambiguous naming, missing metadata, or conflicting information\n\n9. If publication text and filenames conflict, prioritize the publication/PRIDE metadata, but mention the conflict in reasoning.\n\n10. Return ONLY valid JSON. Do not include markdown, comments, or explanatory text outside the JSON.\n\n---\n\nOUTPUT JSON STRUCTURE\n\n{\n  "clusters": [\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_1",\n      "cluster_label": "yeast_glucose_bs3",\n      "description": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae samples grown in glucose-rich medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "organism": "Saccharomyces cerevisiae",\n      "taxid": "NCBITaxon:4932",\n      "crosslinker": "BS3",\n      "biological_condition": "glucose-rich medium",\n      "files": [\n        "YeastGlu_R1.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_R2.raw",\n        "YeastGlu_fraction_01.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "high",\n      "evidence_quote": "Yeast cells were cultured in glucose- or glycerol-containing medium and crosslinked with BS3.",\n      "reasoning": "The filenames contain Glu and the publication states that glucose-grown yeast samples were crosslinked with BS3."\n    },\n    {\n      "cluster_id": "cluster_0",\n      "cluster_label": "unassigned",\n      "description": "Files that could not be confidently assigned to a biological XL-MS condition.",\n      "organism": null,\n      "taxid": null,\n      "crosslinker": null,\n      "biological_condition": null,\n      "files": [\n        "calibrant.raw",\n        "QC.raw"\n      ],\n      "confidence": "low",\n      "evidence_quote": null,\n      "reasoning": "These files appear to be QC or calibration runs rather than biological samples."\n    }\n  ],\n  "summary": {\n    "n_input_files": 5,\n    "n_assigned_files": 3,\n    "n_unassigned_files": 2,\n    "n_biological_clusters": 1,\n    "overall_confidence": "high"\n  }\n}'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
