2026-06-30 15:13:40,782 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-e81e9c66-7150-4129-8daa-70d78c947182', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms from the provided publication text and/or PRIDE project description.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: disease\nsdrf_column: characteristics[disease]\nontology: EFO:0000408, MONDO, DOID, PATO\ndescription: Disease state of the sample.\ninstructions: For healthy/normal samples use "normal" (PATO:0000461). For diseased\n  samples use MONDO or DOID terms. Return null only if completely unstated.\n  Most XL-MS samples are "normal" unless explicitly studying a disease model.\n\nterm: phenotype\nsdrf_column: characteristics[phenotype]\nontology: EFO:0000651, PATO, EFO\ndescription: Observable characteristics or traits (drug sensitivity, molecular markers,\n  expression phenotypes, environmental responses).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. This captures how the sample behaves/appears\n  (distinct from disease diagnosis).\n\nterm: treatment\nsdrf_column: characteristics[treatment]\nontology: EFO:0000727, NCIT, EFO\ndescription: Treatment or perturbation applied to the sample (drug, stimulus,\n  environmental stress, growth condition change).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated or not applicable. Examples: "doxycycline\n  treatment", "heat shock", "DMSO vehicle control".\n\nterm: compound\nsdrf_column: characteristics[compound]\nontology: CHEBI:24431, CHEBI, NCIT, EFO\ndescription: Chemical compound or drug applied to the sample.\ninstructions: Use ChEBI if possible. Return null if no compound treatment. Do NOT\n  include the crosslinking reagent here — that goes in comment[cross-linker].\n\nterm: dose\nsdrf_column: characteristics[dose]\nontology: NCIT:C25488\ndescription: Dose or concentration of compound treatment.\npattern: number + unit (e.g. 10 mg/kg, 50 uM, 100 nM, 10 mg/mL)\ninstructions: Return null if no compound treatment or dose not stated.\n\nterm: genetic modification\nsdrf_column: characteristics[genetic modification]\nontology: EFO:0000510, EFO\ndescription: Method of genetic modification (knockout, knockdown, overexpression,\n  transduction). Describes HOW the modification was done.\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: perturbation\nsdrf_column: characteristics[perturbation]\nontology: PRIDE:0000700\ndescription: Type of perturbation applied (environmental/experimental perturbation).\ninstructions: Return null if not stated or not applicable.\n\nterm: depletion\nsdrf_column: characteristics[depletion]\nontology: PRIDE:0000020\ndescription: Whether abundant protein depletion was performed.\nallowed_values: no depletion, depletion, not applicable\ninstructions: Return "no depletion" if not mentioned. Return "depletion" if\n  high-abundance protein removal is described (e.g. albumin depletion).\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nCharacterizing protein conformers by cross-linking mass spectrometry and pattern recognition\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nMotivation: Chemical cross-linking coupled to mass spectrometry (XLMS) emerged as a powerful technique for studying protein structures and large-scale protein-protein interactions. Nonetheless, XLMS lacks software tailored toward dealing with multiple conformers; this scenario can lead to high-quality identifications that are mutually exclusive. This limitation hampers the applicability of XLMS in structural experiments of dynamic protein systems, where less abundant conformers of the target protein are expected in sample. Results: We present QUIN-XL, a software that uses unsupervised clustering to group cross-link identifications by their quantitative profile across multiple samples. QUIN-XL highlights regions of the protein or system presenting changes in its conformation when comparing different biological conditions. We demonstrate the usefulness of our software by revisiting the HSP90 protein, comparing three of its different conformers. QUIN-XL’s clusters correlate directly to known protein 3D structures of the conformers and therefore validates our software.\n\n[METHODS]\nThe HSP90 protein used for the experiments was the human one, analyzed in a purified solution with the nucleotides being added separately afterwards for the formation of complexes. This way, each of the samples should be enriched for a different conformer. The nucleotides were ADP and ATP-γ-S (referenced in the text as ATP), which is a more stable analog of ATP and does not hydrolyze immediately. The cross-linker used was DSS dissolved in DMF (final concentration of 27mmol/L) in a molar excess of 100 times relative to the protein concentration. The cross-link reactions occurred for 2 hours at 27°C. Reducing of the disulfide bonds was done by adding 20μL of a 10mmol/L solution of DTT (prepared in a 100mmol/L buffer solution of ammonium bicarbonate) and incubating it for 30 minutes at 70°C. Alkylation of thiol groups was done by adding 20μL of a 50mmol/L solution of iodoacetamide (prepared in a 100mmol/L ammonium bicarbonate buffer solution) and incubating it for 30 minutes in the dark at room temperature. The cross-linked proteins were digested using trypsin in a proportion of 1:50 (w/w) enzyme:substrate for 18 hours at 37°C. Mass spectrometry data for HSP90 was generated in the following conditions: a) ATP-bound, b) ADP-bound, and c) nucleotide-free (Apo). Digested samples sample were analyzed in two technical replicates using an Orbitrap Fusion Lumos Tribrid (ThermoFisher Scientific) mass spectrometer coupled with an Easy nLC 1200 chromatography system. Each sample was analyzed in 40 minutes chromatography, using linear gradients set as follows: 5 to 30% B (v/v)  (with solvent B: acetonitrile + 0.1% formic acid (v/v)) from 0 to 20 minutes, 30 to 90%  B from 20 to 35 minutes, then 90% B from 35 to 40 minutes. Instrument method was set up in DDA mode, HCD fragmentation with stepped collision energy from 30 to 34. Survey scans were acquired at resolution 60,000. Precursor selection was performed by cycle time with maximum injection time of 250 ms, and 8 seconds dynamic exclusion window. Precursor ions with charge 3+ to 8+ were selected for fragmentation. MS2 spectra were acquired with resolution 30,000.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
